Withdraw a worker from the router when its Pod loses readiness, leaves the
selector, terminates, or is replaced by another identity at the same address.
The discovery tracker is keyed on Pod UID and stays consistent across the
rest of the operator's patches.

THE DEFECT: src/service_discovery.rs (v0.1.16.1) tracks pods in
HashSet<PodInfo> by value and forwards each Kubernetes event to a handler
that filters by selector, parses the status, and registers the resulting
worker URL. A pod that loses readiness, has its selector label removed, or
enters termination is still delivered to the watcher; the handler either
admits it as a fresh worker or silently drops the registration without
ever telling the router. Workers serving to a participant that is no longer
healthy stay selectable, and a same-name replacement at the same address
gets admitted alongside the previous owner -- a later Delete event for the
old UID then withdraws the current occupant from the router.

THE FIX: the tracker becomes HashMap<String, PodInfo> keyed on Pod UID, the
PodInfo struct gains a `uid` field, and handle_watch_event gains four
withdrawal decisions (readiness loss, selector miss, terminating status,
relist absence) plus a take-over step that withdraws a previous owner at
the same address before admitting its replacement. A late Delete for a
replaced UID finds nothing to withdraw and cannot reach the current
occupant. Pods whose UID is absent or empty are refused at from_pod() so
they cannot be admitted as workers that can never be reliably removed.

Equality and hashing for PodInfo intentionally ignore `uid`: the tracker
keys on UID separately, but value equality inside tests and downstream
consumers compares the worker itself. A manual PartialEq / Eq / Hash pair
on the routing-relevant fields (name, ip, status, is_ready, pod_type,
bootstrap_port) keeps the Eq/Hash contract sound -- a derive that included
uid would compare uids that are deliberately distinct on identical workers.
Eleven new tests cover the withdrawal and take-over paths; they use a
StubRouter (implements WorkerManagement and RouterTrait, routing methods
return 501) instead of a real Router, because Router::add_worker does an
HTTP health check that fails against the synthetic addresses the tests
construct. The 11 tests assert on tracker state, not on router HTTP
behaviour, and the stub is sufficient.

AGAINST: vllm-project/router v0.1.16.1 (commit
91b65ee4bdfbe98863a32a2205dced5dbb249ffe) with PR 266 (EPD, commit
516f1cb90eefe565c9fc894d72c8748eae25085b) cherry-picked on top
(0001-epd-routing.patch), plus the lazy-Mooncake patch (0002) and the
read-only observer patch (0003). The defect is present in the same shape
on the default branch at the time of writing. The image build applies
this patch with git apply after 0001, 0002, 0003; a pin whose hunks no
longer match fails loudly and surfaces at build time, not in a cluster.

WHEN TO REMOVE: when upstream restructures service discovery so that a
pod losing readiness, leaving the selector, or entering termination
withdraws the worker from the router. Until then the EPD + Kubernetes
service discovery path this patch fixes is a regression vs the operator's
prior baseline.

